course: language
lesson: 36
title: Grammar puzzles, part two
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Words are like Lego: you can flip them, shuffle their letters, read them backwards and give them new jobs, and every puzzle has an exact answer you can check letter by letter.
explain: Picture a wet afternoon: you write "level" on a steamy window and read it from the other side of the glass, and it's still "level"; that is a palindrome.
explain: Shuffle the letters of "listen" and you get "silent"; that's an anagram, and the letters must be used exactly once each, no more and no less.
explain: One word can do several jobs: "fast" can be a describing word, a how-word, a doing-word or a naming-word, and only the sentence tells you which.
explain: The trick with every puzzle is to slow down, count carefully and test your answer before you shout it out.
fact: In the active voice the doer comes first: "The cat chased the mouse"; in the passive voice the thing the action is done to comes first, and "by" shows the doer: "The mouse was chased by the cat".
fact: A palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards: level, radar, kayak, racecar; "Was it a car or a cat I saw?" is a palindrome sentence if you ignore spaces and punctuation.
fact: An anagram is a word made by rearranging all the letters of another word: "listen" gives "silent", "tinsel" and "enlist"; "night" gives "thing", and "earth" gives "heart".
fact: A pangram is a sentence that uses every letter of the alphabet at least once; "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog" is a famous one, used for testing typewriters and fonts.
fact: "Strengths" has nine letters and only one vowel letter, e.
fact: "Facetious" (jokey at the wrong moment) has the vowels a, e, i, o, u once each, in alphabetical order.
fact: "Rhythm", "myth", "gym", "fly" and "sky" have no a, e, i, o or u; in them the letter y does the job of a vowel.
fact: "Fast" can be an adjective ("a fast car"), an adverb ("he runs fast"), a verb meaning go without food ("I fast on holy days") and a noun ("a three-day fast").
fact: "Uncopyrightable" has 15 letters and not one of them repeats; a word with no repeated letters is called an isogram.
fact: To move "I will see you tomorrow" into the past, change both the verb and the time word: "I saw you yesterday".
fact: Some words mean their own opposite: "dust" can mean take dust off (dust the shelf) or put powder on (dust the cake with sugar), and "left" can mean gone (he left) or still here (one biscuit left).
exercise: Turn "The cat chased the mouse" into the passive voice. | The mouse was chased by the cat | The mouse chased the cat | The cat was chased by the mouse | The cat is chasing the mouse
exercise: In the passive "The mouse was chased by the cat", which little word shows who did the chasing? | By | Was | The | Chased
exercise: What do level, radar, kayak and racecar have in common? | They are palindromes | They all name ways of travelling | They are all anagrams of each other | They each have exactly five letters
exercise: Which word is a palindrome? | Refer | River | Rover | Ruler
exercise: Which word is an anagram of "listen"? | Silent | Lists | Glisten | Tense
exercise: Which pair are anagrams of each other? | Earth and heart | Earth and hearth | Heart and hearty | Earth and death
exercise: What is special about "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog"? | It uses every letter of the alphabet | It is a palindrome when read backwards | It has exactly one word for each vowel | It is the oldest sentence in English
exercise: Which nine-letter word has only one vowel letter? | Strengths | Sandwich | Breakfast | Christmas
exercise: Which word has all five vowels, a, e, i, o, u, in alphabetical order? | Facetious | Education | Favourite | Delicious
exercise: Which word has no a, e, i, o or u? | Rhythm | Rhyme | Rabbit | Robin
exercise: What part of speech is "fast" in "a three-day fast"? | A noun | A verb | An adverb | An adjective
exercise: What part of speech is "fast" in "he runs fast"? | An adverb | A noun | An adjective | A verb
exercise: What is unusual about "uncopyrightable"? | None of its 15 letters repeats | It reads the same both ways | It has no vowels in it | It has every letter of the alphabet
exercise: Change "I will see you tomorrow" into the past so it still makes sense. | I saw you yesterday | I saw you tomorrow | I will see you yesterday | I seed you yesterday
exercise: "Dust the shelf" and "dust the cake with sugar". What's odd about "dust"? | It can mean take off or put on | It is spelled differently in each one | It is only a noun in both sentences | It means exactly the same in both
exercise: What is a word with no repeated letters called? | An isogram | A palindrome | An anagram | A pangram
test: Your mate goes "the mouse was chased by the cat means the mouse did the chasing, innit." What's right? | No, the cat still did the chasing; "by the cat" shows the doer | Yes, whoever comes first always does the action | Yes, passive sentences swap who does what | No, nobody did any chasing in that sentence
test: Freya writes "Was it a car or a cat I saw?" backwards, ignoring spaces. What happens? | She gets the same letters in the same order | She gets a totally different sentence | She gets "I saw a cat" and nothing else | The letters run out halfway through
test: Your Scouse cousin says "made an anagram of 'night', la, it's 'thing', dead easy." ("La" means mate, "dead" means very.) Is he right? | Yes, same five letters in a new order | No, "thing" has one letter too many | No, anagrams must keep the first letter | No, the letters must stay in their places
test: What would happen if a pangram left out the letter z? | It would no longer be a pangram | It would still be a pangram, z doesn't count | It would turn into a palindrome | It would become an isogram
test: A Gen Z gamer says "no word can be nine letters with one vowel, that's cap." ("Cap" means a lie.) Which word proves him wrong? | Strengths | Spaghetti | Scratched | Scrumptious
test: Your little bro writes "myth" and says "you can't spell without a vowel letter, dummy." What's true? | In "myth" the y does the job of a vowel | He's right, "myth" is spelled wrongly | "Myth" really has a hidden e | Words without vowels are only in Welsh
test: Your nan from Glasgow says "Ah'm fasting the day, hen." ("The day" means today; "hen" means dear.) What job is "fasting" doing? | A verb: going without food | A noun: a quick race | An adjective: describing speed | An adverb: telling how fast
test: Kai changes "I will see you tomorrow" into "I will see you yesterday". What did he forget? | To change the verb into the past as well | To change "tomorrow" into "today" | To add a question mark at the end | Nothing, his sentence makes perfect sense
test: Mum says "Take the last biscuit, there's one left." Dad says "Your brother left an hour ago." What's going on with "left"? | It means "still here" in one and "gone" in the other | It means exactly the same in both | It means the opposite of right in both | It is a spelling mistake in one of them
test: Your mate says "racecar backwards is racecra, so it's not a palindrome." What did he do wrong? | He made a mistake reversing it; it reads "racecar" | He's right, it isn't a palindrome | Palindromes only work with three letters | Racecar isn't a real word, so it can't count
test: Which of these is an isogram, with no letter used twice? | Lamp | Letter | Banana | Moon
exam: Render the active sentence "The cat chased the mouse" in the passive voice. | The mouse was chased by the cat | The cat was chased by the mouse | The mouse chased the cat | The cat had been chasing the mouse
exam: Identify the term designating a word or phrase which reads identically in both directions. | A palindrome | An anagram | A pangram | An isogram
exam: Which of the following words constitutes an anagram of "listen"? | Enlist | Lessen | Linnet | Glisten
exam: Ascertain the grammatical function of "fast" in the sentence "He runs fast". | Adverb | Noun | Verb | Adjective
